1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,380
Hello, Kira, how are you?

2
00:00:02,380 --> 00:00:04,800
Fine thanks, Paul, how are you?

3
00:00:04,800 --> 00:00:05,401
Well, thanks.

4
00:00:05,401 --> 00:00:06,904
It's good to see you.

5
00:00:06,904 --> 00:00:09,910
It must be twelve months since you did our course?

6
00:00:09,910 --> 00:00:10,648
That's right.

7
00:00:10,648 --> 00:00:13,600
It's nice to come back and say hello.

8
00:00:13,600 --> 00:00:15,770
What course did you enrol in?

9
00:00:15,770 --> 00:00:19,750
Actually, I went straight into third year Pharmacy.

10
00:00:19,750 --> 00:00:22,197
They credited me with two years,

11
00:00:22,197 --> 00:00:25,460
which probably made it more difficult for me.

12
00:00:25,460 --> 00:00:28,509
On the other hand, you were lucky to be granted credits.

13
00:00:28,509 --> 00:00:30,450
Is that why you chose the course?

14
00:00:30,450 --> 00:00:30,792
Yes.

15
00:00:30,792 --> 00:00:35,585
And, as I'd already finished a course in it in my country, I thought

16
00:00:35,585 --> 00:00:39,350
it would be easier if I studied something I already knew.

17
00:00:39,350 --> 00:00:41,600
I didn't realise you went into third year.

18
00:00:41,600 --> 00:00:43,210
I thought you started in first year.

19
00:00:43,210 --> 00:00:45,050
No wonder it wonder it was so hard!

20
00:00:45,050 --> 00:00:50,204
And what do you think is one of the big differences between studying at a university here

21
00:00:50,204 --> 00:00:51,720
and studying in your country?

22
00:00:51,720 --> 00:00:55,293
Well, I've found it very difficult to write assignments,

23
00:00:55,293 --> 00:00:59,660
because I wasn't familiar with that aspect of the system here.

24
00:00:59,660 --> 00:01:03,570
The main problem is that the lecturers expect you to be critical.

25
00:01:03,570 --> 00:01:06,137
That made me feel really terrible.

26
00:01:06,137 --> 00:01:09,560
I thought "How can I possibly do it?

27
00:01:09,560 --> 00:01:14,521
How can I comment on someone else's research when they probably spent five years

28
00:01:14,521 --> 00:01:15,230
doing it?"

29
00:01:15,230 --> 00:01:20,885
I think lot of people who come from overseas countries have similar problems.

30
00:01:20,885 --> 00:01:24,800
But after a while it became easier for me.

31
00:01:24,800 --> 00:01:29,392
People expect you to have problems with the process of reading and writing,

32
00:01:29,392 --> 00:01:34,337
but, in fact, it is more a question of altering your viewpoint towards academic

33
00:01:34,337 --> 00:01:34,690
study.

34
00:01:34,690 --> 00:01:37,321
How was the content of the lectures?

35
00:01:37,321 --> 00:01:39,200
Was it easy for you?

36
00:01:39,200 --> 00:01:42,890
I didn't really have many problems understanding lectures.

37
00:01:42,890 --> 00:01:46,060
The content was very similar to what I'd studied before.

38
00:01:46,060 --> 00:01:48,179
And what about the lecturers themselves?

39
00:01:48,179 --> 00:01:51,710
Are they essentially the same as lecturers in your country?

40
00:01:51,710 --> 00:01:53,140
Well actually, no.

41
00:01:53,140 --> 00:01:56,000
Here, they're much easier to approach.

42
00:01:56,000 --> 00:02:00,230
After every lecture you can go and ask them something you didn't understand.

43
00:02:00,230 --> 00:02:03,057
Or you can make an appointment and talk to them.

44
00:02:03,057 --> 00:02:04,470
About anything in the course.

45
00:02:04,470 --> 00:02:08,535
Maybe you found them different because you're a more mature student now, whereas when

46
00:02:08,535 --> 00:02:12,310
you were studying in your country you were younger and not so assertive.

47
00:02:12,310 --> 00:02:15,185
No, I don't think that's the difference.

48
00:02:15,185 --> 00:02:18,060
Most of the students here do it.

49
00:02:18,060 --> 00:02:22,337
In my faculty, they all seem to make appointments -- usually to talk about

50
00:02:22,337 --> 00:02:24,640
something in the course that's worrying them,

51
00:02:24,640 --> 00:02:30,067
but sometimes just about something that might really interest them, something they might want

52
00:02:30,067 --> 00:02:31,230
to specialise in.

53
00:02:31,230 --> 00:02:36,740
The lecturers must set aside certain times every week when they're available for students.

54
00:02:36,740 --> 00:02:38,380
That's good to hear.

55
00:02:38,380 --> 00:02:40,430
And how was your timetable?

56
00:02:40,430 --> 00:02:42,890
Was it a very busy year?

57
00:02:42,890 --> 00:02:44,156
Very, very busy.

58
00:02:44,156 --> 00:02:46,687
They make you work very hard.

59
00:02:46,687 --> 00:02:51,750
Apart from lectures, we had practical sessions in a lot of subjects.

60
00:02:51,750 --> 00:02:53,916
We did these in small groups.

61
00:02:53,916 --> 00:02:58,970
I had to go and work four hours every week in a community pharmacy.

62
00:02:58,970 --> 00:03:03,800
Actually, I enjoyed this very much-meeting new people all the time.

63
00:03:03,800 --> 00:03:08,182
Then in second semester, we had to get experience in hospital dispensaries,

64
00:03:08,182 --> 00:03:12,564
so every second day we went to one of the big hospitals

65
00:03:12,564 --> 00:03:13,660
and worked there.

66
00:03:13,660 --> 00:03:16,666
And on top of all that we had our assignments,

67
00:03:16,666 --> 00:03:18,770
which took me a lot of time.

68
00:03:18,770 --> 00:03:24,083
Oh, I nearly forgot, between first and second semesters, we had to work full-time

69
00:03:24,083 --> 00:03:26,360
for two weeks in a hospital.

70
00:03:26,360 --> 00:03:28,360
That does sound a very heavy year.

71
00:03:28,360 --> 00:03:30,930
So are you pleased now that you did it?

72
00:03:30,930 --> 00:03:32,930
Do you feel some sense of achievement?

73
00:03:32,930 --> 00:03:35,730
Yeah, I do feel much more confident,

74
00:03:35,730 --> 00:03:38,930
which I suppose is the most important thing.

75
00:03:38,930 --> 00:03:42,680
And have you got any recommendations for people who are studying from overseas?

76
00:03:42,680 --> 00:03:46,150
Well, I suppose they need very good English.

77
00:03:46,150 --> 00:03:50,638
It would be much better if they spent more time learning English before they

78
00:03:50,638 --> 00:03:51,600
enter the university,

79
00:03:51,600 --> 00:03:56,325
because you can be in big trouble if you don't understand what people are saying

80
00:03:56,325 --> 00:03:58,530
and you haven't got time to translate.

81
00:03:58,530 --> 00:03:59,364
Anything else?

82
00:03:59,364 --> 00:04:05,199
Well, as I said before, the biggest problem for me was a lack of

83
00:04:05,199 --> 00:04:07,700
familiarity with the education system here.

84
00:04:07,700 --> 00:04:11,113
It sounds as if it was a real challenge.

85
00:04:11,113 --> 00:04:11,872
Congratulations, Kira.

86
00:04:11,872 --> 00:04:12,630
Thanks, Paul.
